00:01:28.559 --> 00:01:34.800
For my presentation today, I'm going to talk about the Cagey Fields in the north-west

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:40.599
of Ireland, one of the largest Neolithic sites in the world.

00:01:40.599 --> 00:01:45.279
I recently visited this site and observed the work that is currently being done by a

00:01:45.279 --> 00:01:47.959
team of archaeologists there.

00:01:47.959 --> 00:01:54.760
The site was first discovered in the 1930s by a local teacher, Patrick Caulfield.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:02:00.279
He noticed that when local people were digging in the bog, they were constantly hitting against

00:02:00.279 --> 00:02:03.599
what seemed to be rows of stones.

00:02:03.599 --> 00:02:09.360
He realised that these must be walls and that they must be thousands of years old for them

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.800
to predate the bog which subsequently grew over them.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:19.080
He wrote to the National Museum in Dublin to ask them to investigate, but no one took

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:20.639
him seriously.

00:02:20.639 --> 00:02:26.720
It wasn't until 40 years later when Patrick Caulfield's son, Seamus, who had become

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:31.259
an archaeologist by then, began to explore further.

00:02:31.259 --> 00:02:37.839
He inserted iron probes into the bog to map the formation of the stones, a traditional

00:02:37.839 --> 00:02:43.039
method which local people had always used for finding fuel buried in the bog for thousands

00:02:43.039 --> 00:02:45.160
of years.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:52.559
Carbon dating later proved that the site was over 5,000 years old and was the largest Neolithic

00:02:52.559 --> 00:02:55.360
site in Ireland.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:01.080
Thanks to the bog which covers the area, the remains of the settlement at Cagey Fields,

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:06.360
which is over 5,000 years old, are extremely well preserved.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:13.520
A bog is 90% water, its soil so saturated that when the grasses and heathers that grow

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:20.199
on its surface die, they don't fully decay but accumulate in layers.

00:03:20.199 --> 00:03:25.080
Objects remain so well preserved in these conditions because of the acidity of the peat

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.919
and the deficiency of oxygen.

00:03:27.919 --> 00:03:34.039
At least 175 days of rain a year are required for this to happen.

00:03:34.039 --> 00:03:44.360
This part of Ireland gets an average of 225 days.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:49.039
The Neolithic farmers at Cagey would have enjoyed several centuries of relative peace

00:03:49.039 --> 00:03:51.320
and stability.

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:55.839
Neolithic farmers generally lived in larger communities than their predecessors, with

00:03:55.839 --> 00:04:00.080
a number of houses built around a community building.

00:04:00.119 --> 00:04:06.679
As they lived in permanent settlements, Neolithic farmers were able to build bigger houses.

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:12.399
These weren't round, as people often assume, but rectangular, with a small hole in the

00:04:12.399 --> 00:04:15.880
roof that allowed smoke to escape.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:21.200
This is one of many innovations and indicates that the Neolithic farmers were the first

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.640
people to cook indoors.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:29.559
Another new technology that Neolithic settlers brought to Ireland was pottery.

00:04:29.559 --> 00:04:35.000
Fragments of Neolithic pots have been found in Cagey and elsewhere in Ireland.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:40.679
The pots were used for many things, as well as for storing food, pots were filled with

00:04:40.679 --> 00:04:47.279
a small amount of fat, and when this was set alight they served as lamps.

00:04:47.279 --> 00:04:53.880
It's thought that the Cagey fields were mainly used as paddocks for animals to graze in.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:59.079
Evidence from the Cagey fields suggests that each plot of land was of a suitable size to

00:04:59.079 --> 00:05:02.079
sustain an extended family.

00:05:02.079 --> 00:05:07.640
They may have used a system of rotational grazing in order to prevent overgrazing and

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:11.600
to allow for plant recovery and regrowth.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:15.619
This must have been a year-round activity, as no structures have been found which would

00:05:15.619 --> 00:05:18.959
have been used to shelter animals in the winter.

00:05:18.959 --> 00:05:26.559
However, archaeologists believe that this way of life at Cagey ceased abruptly.

00:05:26.559 --> 00:05:27.959
Why was this?

00:05:27.959 --> 00:05:32.760
Well, several factors may have contributed to the changing circumstances.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:38.320
The soil would have become less productive and led to the abandonment of farming.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:43.839
The crop rotation system was partly responsible for this, as it would have been very intensive

00:05:43.839 --> 00:05:46.200
and was not sustainable.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:48.959
But there were also climatic pressures too.

00:05:48.959 --> 00:05:54.880
The farmers at Cagey would have enjoyed a relatively dry period, but this began to change

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.399
and the conditions became wetter as there was a lot more rain.

00:05:59.399 --> 00:06:06.519
It was these conditions that encouraged the bog to form over the area which survives today.

00:06:06.519 --> 00:06:11.799
So now I'd like to show you some...

00:06:11.799 --> 00:06:14.359
That is the end of Part 4.

